[25.768787, "o", "Found the actual fault. Let me confirm the repair path before changing anything.\r\n"]
[26.946889, "o", "\u001b[0m$ \u001b[0mpython3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"\r\nLooking in links: /tmp/tmpif0yqsep\r\nRequirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)\r\nWARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager, possibly rendering your system unusable.It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv. Use the --root-user-action option if you know what you are doing and want to suppress this warning.\r\nexit=0\r\n"]
[27.871673, "o", "> build · deepseek-v4.1-flash\r\n"]
[30.044763, "o", "`ensurepip` was fooled by the leftover metadata. The package files are gone while `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` remains, so pip \"thinks\" it's installed. I'll move that orphaned metadata aside and reinstall pip from the bundled wheel.\r\n"]
[31.265682, "o", "\u001b[0m$ \u001b[0mmkdir -p /tmp/opencode/pip-repair-backup && mv /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info /tmp/opencode/pip-repair-backup/ && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"\r\nLooking in links: /tmp/tmpfxqwzksf\r\nProcessing /tmp/tmpfxqwzksf/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl\r\nInstalling collected packages: pip\r\nSuccessfully installed pip-24.3.1\r\nWARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager, possibly rendering your system unusable.It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv. Use the --root-user-action option if you know what you are doing and want to suppress this warning.\r\nexit=0\r\n"]
[32.382905, "o", "> build · deepseek-v4.1-flash\r\n"]
[34.369529, "o", "\u001b[0m$ \u001b[0mpip --version 2>&1; pip3 --version 2>&1; python3 -m pip --version 2>&1; echo \"--- pip location ---\"; python3 -c \"import pip; print(pip.__file__, pip.__version__)\" 2>&1\r\npip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\r\npip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\r\npip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\r\n--- pip location ---\r\n/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/__init__.py 24.3.1\r\n"]
[34.397376, "o", "\u001b[0m$ \u001b[0mpip install --target /tmp/opencode/pip-smoke --no-cache-dir --quiet six 2>&1; echo \"install_exit=$?\"; python3 -c \"import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/tmp/opencode/pip-smoke'); import six; print('six', six.__version__)\" 2>&1\r\nWARNING: Running pip as the 'root' user can result in broken permissions and conflicting behaviour with the system package manager, possibly rendering your system unusable.It is recommended to use a virtual environment instead: https://pip.pypa.io/warnings/venv. Use the --root-user-action option if you know what you are doing and want to suppress this warning.\r\n\r\n[notice] A new release of pip is available: 24.3.1 -> 26.2.2\r\n[notice] To update, run: pip install --upgrade pip\r\ninstall_exit=0\r\nsix 1.17.0\r\n"]
[35.51919, "o", "> build · deepseek-v4.1-flash\r\n"]
[36.560434, "o", "\u001b[0m$ \u001b[0mrm -rf /tmp/opencode/pip-smoke; ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/\r\nREADME.txt\r\npip\r\npip-24.3.1.dist-info\r\n"]
[37.32004, "o", "> build · deepseek-v4.1-flash\r\n"]
[39.143002, "o", "Fixed — pip is working again.\r\n\r\n## What was wrong\r\n\r\n`/usr/local/bin/python3` (Python 3.13.1) had **no `pip` package installed at all**, but its metadata folder was left behind:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/\r\n├── README.txt\r\n└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info/   ← metadata said \"pip is installed\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `pip/` code directory was missing, so every invocation failed with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is why `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` didn't help at first — it saw the leftover `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` and concluded pip was already installed.\r\n\r\n## What I did\r\n\r\n1. Moved the orphaned metadata to `/tmp/opencode/pip-repair-backup/` (kept as a backup, safe to delete).\r\n2. Reinstalled pip from Python's own bundled wheel — no network needed:\r\n   ```\r\n   python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n## Verified\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ pip --version\r\npip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)\r\n```\r\n\r\nI also did a real en"]
[39.14304, "o", "d-to-end install test (`six` into a throwaway target dir), which succeeded, then removed it.\r\n\r\n## One recommendation\r\n\r\nInstalling system-wide works now, but on Debian with a source-built Python it's easy to break like this again. For project work, prefer a virtual environment:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npython3 -m venv .venv\r\nsource .venv/bin/activate\r\npip install <package>\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote: `/usr/bin/python3` (Debian's 3.11) is a separate interpreter and has no pip — that's normal for Debian; it would come from apt's `python3-pip`. The `python3`/`pip` on your `PATH` resolve to `/usr/local/bin` 3.13, which is what I repaired.\r\n"]
[39.206509, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@8709aded7731:/app# "]
